---
/**
 * ============================================================================
 * The first-visit intro overlay.
 *
 * Markup only. Nothing here is visible, and nothing here downloads, unless the
 * gate in Base.astro has already put data-intro="run" on <html>. That decision
 * happens in a blocking script in <head>, before this markup is parsed, so the
 * overlay is either painted on the very first frame or it never exists.
 *
 * TWO IMAGES CARRY NO src ATTRIBUTE, DELIBERATELY.
 *
 * A returning visitor must download nothing. An <img src> in static markup
 * downloads for everyone, and the sky plate is 39KB and the lockup is 15KB, so
 * that is 54KB charged to people who will never see a frame of this. The gate
 * picks the right plate for the viewport, preloads it, and writes the URL onto
 * <html>; src/scripts/intro.ts copies it onto the elements. What is left in the
 * HTML for a returning visitor is this markup, about 380 bytes, and the CSS
 * below.
 *
 * THE LOCKUP IS CHARCOAL, NOT BONE. This is a correction to the brief, which
 * asked for lockup-bone.svg over a washed sky. lockup-bone.svg is #F2EFE9 and
 * the wash it would land on is --paper, #F4F1EA: two shades of the same bone,
 * an invisible logo. Charcoal on paper is the pairing the nav and the Open
 * Graph card already use, and it crossfades onto a paper homepage cleanly.
 *
 * The camera is a real perspective camera rather than a scale() on a flat
 * image. For Phase A, one plane, the two are indistinguishable. For Phase B
 * they are not: giving each cut layer its own translateZ inside this same
 * container is the whole of the parallax work, and none of this structure
 * changes. See CC-PROMPT-INTRO-ANIMATION.md.
 * ========================================================================= */
---

<div class="intro" data-intro-overlay aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="intro-stage">
    <div class="intro-drift" data-intro-drift>
      <div class="intro-dolly" data-intro-dolly>
        <img class="intro-plate" data-intro-plate alt="" decoding="async" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="intro-edge" data-intro-edge></div>
  <div class="intro-floor" data-intro-floor></div>
  <div class="intro-wash" data-intro-wash></div>

  <div class="intro-logo" data-intro-logo>
    <img data-intro-lockup alt="" decoding="async" />
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /* Hidden by default and shown only by the gate's attribute. The default is
     the safe one: no attribute, no overlay, homepage as normal. This rule and
     the markup travel in the same HTML document, so the CSS cannot arrive
     without the markup it governs or the other way round. */
  .intro {
    display: none;
  }
  :global(html[data-intro="run"]) .intro {
    display: block;
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 9999;
    overflow: hidden;

    /* The plate's own sky, so the very first frame is the right blue rather
       than white. Sampled from the illustration at 50% 25%. Solid colour
       paints instantly and costs nothing, which matters because the plate
       itself is still arriving. */
    background: #1579db;

    /* Absorb the first click rather than letting it through to a CTA. The
       first input skips the intro; the second one books the call. A click
       that does both at once is a click nobody asked for. */
    pointer-events: auto;
    touch-action: none;
  }

  .intro-stage {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    /* The vanishing point, not the centre of the frame. 42% down the viewport
       is the open sky between the seat backs, which is where the camera is
       flying. Pitching it above centre also carries the legs and the seat
       edges down and out of frame instead of through the lens, which is the
       cheap half of the "you fly through your own legs" problem. */
    perspective: 1000px;
    perspective-origin: 50% 42%;
  }

  /* preserve-3d is load-bearing: without it the dolly's translateZ is
     flattened by this element and the perspective above does nothing. */
  .intro-drift {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    transform-style: preserve-3d;
  }

  .intro-dolly {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    transform-style: preserve-3d;
  }

  /* Overscanned by 4%. The drift moves the frame 1.5% and rolls it 0.4deg;
     that roll displaces a corner by about another 0.4%, so 3% is the floor and
     4% is the floor plus a margin. It was 6%, which was a guess, and the guess
     cost real composition: `cover` already crops a 1.78 plate into a 1.6
     viewport, and every extra point of overscan was eating further into the
     two windows that make the shot read as a cabin. This is layout, set once,
     never animated. */
  .intro-plate {
    position: absolute;
    inset: -4%;
    width: 108%;
    height: 108%;
    /* global.css sets `img, svg { max-width: 100% }`, which silently clamped
       this back to 100% of the stage while the element stayed offset at -6%.
       The result was an 86px strip of bare background down the right edge of
       every desktop frame, which looked exactly like an image that had failed
       to cover rather than a rule from three files away. Overscan is the whole
       point of this box, so it opts out by name. */
    max-width: none;
    max-height: none;
    object-fit: cover;
    /* The illustration's subject sits centre-bottom. On a viewport far wider
       or taller than the plate, cover crops from the edges and this keeps the
       legs and both windows in frame. */
    object-position: 50% 50%;
  }

  /* THERE IS NO SPEED SMEAR, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
     The brief asks for a radial motion blur or a zoom streak over the dolly.
     filter: blur() on a full viewport surface is a per-frame paint over a
     million pixels and the performance budget rules it out, so this was a
     second copy of the same plate scaled 1.055 and faded in at peak velocity:
     transform and opacity only, one extra composited layer, no new bytes.

     On a 4x throttled desktop profile it cost 22.2% dropped frames against
     7.6% with it hidden, same page, same runs. Three times the jank. And in a
     nine frame strip of the dolly it is not findable: at 0.34 opacity over an
     image 5% different in scale, there is nothing to see. A second full
     viewport layer cannot reuse the plate's raster, so it is a whole extra
     surface to composite every frame for an effect nobody can point at.

     Requirement 8 of the brief is "60fps or it does not ship", which is not a
     preference. The speed is sold by the dolly curve instead. If this is ever
     revisited, the cheap version is a gradient, not a bitmap.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /* Edges dissolve to paper as they scale past the camera, so the cabin walls
     wash out rather than clipping at the viewport edge. A static gradient with
     only its opacity animated, never a repainted gradient. */
  .intro-edge {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    opacity: 0;
    background: radial-gradient(
      ellipse 78% 72% at 50% 42%,
      rgba(244, 241, 234, 0) 34%,
      rgba(244, 241, 234, 0.55) 68%,
      rgba(244, 241, 234, 0.95) 88%,
      var(--paper) 100%
    );
  }

  /* The full wash the lockup sits on, and the surface that crossfades to the
     homepage. --paper is #F4F1EA; the illustration's own clouds are #FEFAF2.
     Close enough that the clouds appear to become the page. */
  .intro-wash {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    opacity: 0;
    background: var(--paper);
  }
  /* YOU FLY THROUGH YOUR OWN LEGS, and this is the half of that the flat image
     can fix.

     The legs and shoes sit closest to the camera, so the dolly scales them
     hardest and they pass through the lens as two enormous translucent
     sneakers. The brief files this under Phase B, on the grounds that the real
     fix is fading the leg layer once there is a leg layer. There is a cheaper
     one that needs no layers at all: bring paper up from the bottom of the
     frame on its own later, faster curve, so the legs are gone before they are
     big enough to read as legs. The camera keeps flying; the floor is simply
     no longer there to fly through.

     Its own element rather than another stop on .intro-edge, because it has to
     arrive 450ms after the edges do. Starting them together fogs the bottom of
     the frame while the shot is still a cabin. */
  .intro-floor {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    opacity: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(
      to top,
      var(--paper) 0%,
      rgba(244, 241, 234, 0.92) 18%,
      rgba(244, 241, 234, 0.55) 32%,
      rgba(244, 241, 234, 0) 48%
    );
  }

  /* The wash tops out at 0.94, not 1. At a flat 1 the lockup beat is a blank
     sheet of paper and the flight is over a beat early; at 0.94 the blown out
     clouds are still faintly there behind it, which is the difference between
     a washed sky and no sky. */

  .intro-logo {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    opacity: 0;
  }
  .intro-logo img {
    width: min(46vw, 560px);
    /* From the lockup's own viewBox, 4496.7 x 896. Reserves the box before the
       SVG arrives, so the logo beat cannot shift. */
    aspect-ratio: 4496.7 / 896;
    height: auto;
  }
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .intro-logo img {
      width: 74vw;
    }
  }

  /* Belt and braces. The gate already returns before setting the attribute
     when reduced motion is set, so this should be unreachable, and it costs
     one selector to make "unreachable" into "impossible". */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    :global(html[data-intro="run"]) .intro {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
